I(6,[9,10],6,[42],[[45,65]],'unproved claim','les applications ... sont continues','If a locally compact group G acts continuously on a locally compact Hausdorff space E, the translation action on compactly supported continuous functions with its locally convex inductive-limit topology, and on C_0(E) with the sup norm, is jointly continuous. The leaf also begins an unfinished corollary.','The topologies and Hausdorff convention are reconstructed from the proof and the preceding truncated line; the overline K(E) is not defined on the available leaf. This is reused functional-analysis typescript, not crystalline theory.','agrees','First continuity proof is supplied; the norm-completion case is explicitly left to the reader. The corollary is incomplete.')
I(7,[11,12,13],6,[43],[[71,103]],'conjecture','Structures 1,2 ...; 3 et 1 ???','Compare: a two-weight decomposition V=V^{10}⊕V^{01}, a semilinear G_K-action on V, and an integral lattice M with V=M⊗C. Under the two Hodge–Tate weight assumptions, recover the two pieces by invariants and twists; ask what the decomposition and lattice alone determine.','No assertion that every semilinear representation has this decomposition is supported: the margin explicitly has an unresolved implication sign. The completed base and precise reconstruction category are unsettled.','agrees','Notes preserve both the equations and the question marks, including K-dependence and the cancelled sentence.')
I(8,[14],6,[43],[[102,103]],'idea of the earlier run','le plus petit groupe ... tel que G_C contienne les opérations','The earlier run proposes defining the smallest Q_p-algebraic subgroup of GL(M⊗Q_p) containing the Hodge–Tate cocharacter after extension to C, and then documenting its tensors.','This is an earlier-run investigation built on an actual manuscript suggestion. No equality with a Galois or motivic group is asserted.','agrees','The manuscript starts the minimal-group construction but its second diagonal entry remains uncertain.')
I(9,[15,16],6,[44],[[107,129]],'unproved claim','Sp(f(x))=f(Sp(x)); (g∘f)(x)=g(f(x))','For x in a unital complex Banach algebra, holomorphic functional calculus satisfies spectral mapping, invertibility iff 0 is outside f(Sp(x)), and composition.','The ambient Banach-algebra setup is on omitted surrounding typescript leaves; it is a necessary reconstruction.','agrees','Spectral mapping has a proof; composition ends mid-proof. The missing integral sign is a mathematical reading alert.')
I(10,[20,21],6,[46],[[175,197]],'unproved claim','Les deux lemmes suivants figureront au fascicule de Résultats','Cauchy integral measures supported away from a compact set represent Banach-valued holomorphic functions near the compact set or outside a surrounding neighbourhood; a compactly supported measure whose Cauchy transform vanishes near K annihilates holomorphic functions on C minus K vanishing at infinity.','The second lemma needs L in place of the unexplained K or a missing declaration K=L; the first two lemmas are statements, the third has only the start of a Fubini proof.','agrees','The note correctly treats these as reused analytical source material with incomplete arguments.')
I(11,[22,23,24],6,[47,48],[[201,215]],'conjecture','hypersingulières i.e. sans pts d’ordre p','Over an algebraically closed field k of characteristic p>0, if abelian varieties A,B have p-rank zero and isogenous connected formal groups, must A and B be isogenous? In dimension 2 the page reduces this to isogeny with a supersingular elliptic square, reporting Manin’s answer only in p=2.','His definition is p-rank zero. Replacing it by the modern all-slopes-1/2 meaning of supersingular would change the question in dimension ≥3. The title’s broader embedding claim is treated separately.','agrees','The notes retain the word and explicitly report the attribution rather than claiming a proof.')
I(12,[25,26,27,28],6,[48,49],[[217,229]],'unproved claim','est ... isomorphe à un produit E′×F′; famille discrète','The manuscript argues for non-isogenous simple abelian surfaces with the same ordinary-plus-height-two formal group using a two-dimensional p-rank-one locus and a one-dimensional product locus. It strengthens isogenous to E×F (one factor ordinary, the other supersingular) to isomorphic to a product, and asserts isogeny classes descend to finite extensions of a field of definition.','The dimensionally consistent reading of unsure “tte courbe” is an abelian surface. The product-isomorphism assertion is false; its failure does not refute existence of the desired simple examples. One must distinguish a countable union of isogeny correspondences from a closed one-dimensional product locus.','understates','The notes correctly record the strengthened word and cancellation but do not identify the false strengthening. A smooth genus-2 example over Fbar_7 supplies a counterexample.',{26:('corrected','The asserted sharpening from isogeny to isomorphism is false under the natural surface reading; see the explicit genus-2 counterexample and reading alert.'),27:('corrected','The stated field-of-definition argument is not established by the product-locus argument. Preserve it as a separate claim requiring the ordinary-plus-height-two hypothesis.'),28:('kept','The middle paragraph is wholly cancelled; it contributes no live theorem.')})
I(13,[29],6,[48,49],[[217,229]],'idea of the earlier run','image de l’ouvert M′_1 ... E↦E×F','The earlier run proposes constructing the parameter maps for products and analyzing their fibres and isogeny images.','A useful next task, not a manuscript theorem; the isogenous locus cannot be identified with the isomorphic product locus.','agrees','Its stated confidence concerns an investigation. The counterexample sharpens the need for this investigation.')
I(14,[30,31,32,33],6,[50,51],[[233,241]],'unproved claim','On peut tordre ... par n’importe quelle unité theta','Over a suitable finite field with all supersingular elliptic endomorphisms defined, twist its height-two formal p-divisible group by a p-adic quaternion-order unit theta. Choosing theta with transcendental reduced norm prevents the twist, even after finite base extension, from embedding into an abelian variety’s formal group, because its Frobenius eigenvalues would have to be algebraic Weil numbers.','Replace ambiguous det by reduced norm only as an explicit reconstruction; central transcendental units already suffice. The asserted persistence after arbitrary finitely generated field extension requires a separate descent argument and is not proved by the displayed norm test alone.','agrees','The main argument is present but the arbitrary-unit descent continuity and finitely-generated-extension clauses are suppressed; notes accurately call for both.')
I(15,[34,35,36,37,38,39],6,[52,53,54],[[245,318]],'unproved claim','un tore formel; torseur','Deformations of an extension of an étale p-divisible group M_0 by a multiplicative p-divisible group N_0 are a torsor under the formal torus Hom_Zp(T_pM_0,N_0). With the connected part allowed to deform, the extension-deformation space is a torsor over its deformation space under the corresponding universal formal p-divisible group.','The étale hypothesis on M_0 and connected/multiplicative hypotheses are recovered from the structure being discussed, not all stated on the isolated opening 6/053. The two xi_0 symbols on 6/053 must be distinguished before assigning the origin.','agrees','The construction, torus and torsor assertions occur exactly as noted; their compatibility and representability are not proved here.')
I(16,[40,41],6,[55,56],[[321,375]],'unproved claim','v_*(xi)=u^*(xi′); le noyau ... alpha−beta','Given fixed maps u:M→M′ and v:N→N′ between étale and connected p-divisible groups, a morphism of extensions exists precisely when v_*xi=u^*xi′; it is unique when Hom(M,N′)=0. The paired deformation problem is represented by the kernel of the difference of the two induced formal-group maps.','The Hom-vanishing is essential to uniqueness, and holds in the infinitesimal deformation setting with a fixed special-fibre map by rigidity; it is false for arbitrary extensions of arbitrary abelian sheaves.','agrees','The source states equality of extension classes and the kernel construction, with a separate unjustified uniqueness parenthesis.')
