I(39,[97,98],7,[13],[[1027,1057]],'unproved claim','E_2^{p,q}=H_str^p(V^q) ⇒ H^{p+q}(X,M^bullet)','Under the formalization and convergence hypotheses, a bounded-below differential complex gives the displayed spectral sequence from stratification cohomology of its formalized cohomology sheaves; concentration in one row gives the stated comparison.','The symbol ell and the precise general EGA reference are not fully defined on the leaf. Modern derived-category results do not remove its needed finiteness and pro-category assumptions.','agrees','The note keeps the spectral sequence, the one-row condition and the underdefined notation distinct.')
I(40,[99],7,[10,13],[[962,1057]],'idea of the earlier run','EGA O_III ?','The earlier run proposes auditing the bicomplex and R lim terms separately to identify exactly where Mittag–Leffler and boundedness enter.','This is a valid targeted task, not a completed manuscript argument.','agrees','It is prompted by explicit limit and convergence qualifications.')
I(41,[100,101,102],7,[14,15],[[1059,1096]],'unproved claim','cas lisse en car. 0; lemme de Poincaré formel','For a smooth morphism over a Q-scheme, formalization of the relative de Rham complex resolves the structure crystal; hence infinitesimal/stratification cohomology agrees with relative de Rham hypercohomology.','Characteristic zero here must mean all positive integers are invertible, not merely a ring of characteristic zero such as Z. Comparisons for nonsmooth X require completion in a smooth ambient space.','agrees','The smoothness and characteristic-zero restrictions are explicit. The following more general comparison map is not asserted to be an isomorphism.')
I(42,[103],7,[15],[[1077,1096]],'idea of the earlier run','cas lisse en car. 0','On A^1 over F_p, the usual differential complex has H^0=F_p[t^p] and H^1=F_p[t^p]t^(p−1)dt, diagnosing why the characteristic-zero Poincaré argument cannot be transported verbatim.','This elementary calculation tests de Rham exactness; it is not a calculation of the full infinitesimal site.','agrees','The earlier-run example is correctly limited to the displayed differential complex.')
I(43,[104,105,106,107,108,109],7,[16,17],[[1098,1135]],'withdrawn in the same text','C’est en effet faux; la coh ... s’annule pour i>dim X','The hope that non-PD infinitesimal cohomology over characteristic p or W(k) supplies the desired p-adic cohomology is explicitly retracted. The page cites ordinary abelian varieties with vanishing in degrees above their dimension and contrasts this with de Rham and the expected Betti numbers.','The symbol cris on these draft sheets names nilpotent thickenings without PD structures. It must not be read as modern crystalline cohomology, whose ordinary abelian varieties do have top-degree classes. The general finiteness question remains a question.','agrees','Both the earlier hope and the author’s correction are on the contiguous leaves; the note records them accurately.')
I(44,[110],7,[16,17,52,53],[[1098,1135],[1777,1808]],'idea of the earlier run','faux; espoirs','Compare the early failure of the non-PD p-adic site with the later explicit withdrawal of ordinary stratification for characteristic-p Gauss–Manin cohomology.','The two failures concern different constructions; the internal link does not identify their counterexamples or proofs.','agrees','The note is a comparison task and both endpoints contain a correction.')
I(45,[111,112,113,114,115],7,[18,19],[[1137,1173]],'conjecture','changer notre point de vue; considérer des triples','The author proposes a site of triples (U_0,V,i) with U_0 an open of the special fibre, V a weak formal lifting, and i a nilpotent immersion; asks for a topology, recovery of rational Monsky–Washnitzer cohomology on smooth affines, and comparison with de Rham cohomology of proper lifts.','This is a construction programme, not a fully defined site. Tensoring an inverse limit with Q_p cannot be interchanged freely; coefficient and topology choices govern the outcome.','agrees','The notes accurately preserve the tentative topology, test cases and proposed enlargement to nonflat objects.')
I(46,[116,117,118,119,120,121,160],7,[21,22,36],[[1175,1229],[1457,1471]],'unproved claim','Nous avons montré; th. de comparaison','The lecture recapitulates de Rham’s theorem for differentiable manifolds, holomorphic hypercohomology (global forms on Stein manifolds), and Grothendieck’s algebraic–analytic comparison for smooth schemes locally of finite type over C, including nonproper and nonseparated cases under the stated convention.','The corrected date is November–December 1966. The comparison was already circulated in an October 1963 letter and published in 1966; calling the lecture’s recollection an unsettled conjecture would misread its tense.','agrees','The question introducing the proof is answered by the next page’s theorem. Proper comparison is via GAGA; the general proof cites resolution.')
I(47,[122,123,124,127],7,[23,24],[[1231,1267]],'unproved claim','H_DR^0(X) ≃ H^0(X,O_X)^p; ≥2dim Pic_X','The naive algebraic de Rham complex in characteristic p may have infinite-dimensional cohomology on smooth nonproper schemes. Over a perfect field, its degree-zero kernel is the Frobenius-image functions. For smooth proper X the page compares H^1_dR with twice the dimension of Pic^0_red and suggests strictness reflects p-torsion. Characteristic-p traces recover integer fixed-point counts only modulo p.','Perfectness is needed for the displayed identification with literal pth powers, and reduced Picard dimension is distinguished from tangent dimension. The torsion explanation is explicitly tentative; the trace claim presupposes a valid Lefschetz formalism.','agrees','The notes preserve these qualifications and do not supply an unmentioned example for strictness.')
I(48,[125],7,[23],[[1231,1263]],'reading question','cycles; Hodge; Bourbaki','A cancelled paragraph concerns Hodge cohomology, cycles and duality; its complete compatibility statement cannot be recovered from the transcription.','Do not turn surviving nouns or marginal Hartshorne references into a theorem.','agrees','Both deletion and overlapping uncertain writing are material to interpretation.')
I(49,[126],7,[23],[[1246,1253]],'idea of the earlier run','H_DR^0(X)','The F_2 affine-line computation gives kernel F_2[t²] and cokernel tF_2[t²]dt.','This repeats the characteristic-p diagnostic at a concrete prime, not an additional author claim.','agrees','The monomial calculation has been independently redone.')
I(50,[128],7,[24],[[1269,1273]],'unproved claim','un point de rebr. ordinaire; on n’obtient déjà pas ... le premier nb de Betti','The broad assertion that naive Kähler–de Rham hypercohomology may fail for singular complex varieties is correct. The suggested ordinary cuspidal affine line is not a counterexample when the full exterior complex is used: its higher cohomology is zero.','The countercheck uses the natural reading rebroussement and the cusp y²=x³. A different unreadable singularity or a truncated complex would be a different statement; scans must decide the word.','understates','The earlier note properly asks for an explicit ring but leaves the particular example unchallenged. Weighted Euler contraction disproves that example; a published non-quasihomogeneous curve gives a genuine replacement.',{128:('corrected','Retain the general warning, but the ordinary affine cusp has the correct H^1=0 for the full Ω^bullet; do not discard Ω², which is supported at the cusp.')})
I(51,[129,130,131,133,134,135,136,137],7,[24,25,26,27],[[1273,1331]],'conjecture','une construction purement algébrique; une bonne catégorie de coefficients','Seek an algebraic coefficient formalism containing coherent complexes and differential-operator complexes, with Leray spectral sequences, direct and inverse images, exceptional f_! and f^!, duality, Gysin maps and singular homology, compatible with analytic comparison.','These are several requested operations, not a single fully specified category. Modern D-modules, regular holonomic coefficients and coherent duality realize different portions with different hypotheses.','agrees','The notes preserve the actual programme and its named Hartshorne/Bourbaki models, including the tentative sans doute for differential complexes.')
I(52,[132,199],7,[25,46,9,15],[[1276,1307],[1666,1672],[957,1096]],'idea of the earlier run','suites spectrales du type Leray','Make an internal comparison between the requested coefficient/Leray formalism and the formalization spectral sequences, and the later criticism of basic Monsky–Washnitzer complexes.','Both links are explicit earlier-run tasks; no equivalence of the proposed coefficient categories has been established by the notes.','agrees','The three endpoints contain the stated constructions or requests.')
I(53,[138,139,140,141,142],7,[28,29,30],[[1333,1370]],'unproved claim','au moins du point de vue additif; coefficients entiers indépendants de l','For a complex algebraic variety of finite type, étale cohomology with Z_l coefficients agrees with singular integral cohomology tensored with Z_l. Finite generation implies that all such groups determine the integral cohomology additively; ranks are l-independent, torsion occurs at finitely many primes, and an algebraic endomorphism has an integral l-independent characteristic polynomial on the torsion-free part.','The comparison extends to algebraically closed characteristic-zero fields by spreading out and invariance under algebraically closed extension. It does not reconstruct cup products from abstract groups.','agrees','The deleted properness restriction and the explicit additive qualification are retained.')
I(54,[143,144,145,146,147],7,[30,31],[[1371,1391]],'conjecture','on ignore; de Z[1/p]','The lecture asks for general finiteness and l-independence in positive characteristic, proposes Lefschetz-type algebraic correspondences as a route to rational characteristic polynomials, and observes that rational numbers integral at every l≠p still lie only in Z[1/p].','For arbitrary endomorphisms and correspondences, precise properness/smoothness and degreewise versus alternating traces must be distinguished. The partly illegible adjective has no technical role in this reconstruction.','agrees','The notes faithfully retain the historical open status and separate rationality from p-integrality.')
I(55,[148,149,151],7,[32,33],[[1393,1425]],'conjecture','Un argument de Serre; W(k) des vecteurs de Witt','A Weil-type theory with good H^1 dimensions and functorial endomorphism action cannot have Q_p coefficients for every characteristic-p variety: a supersingular elliptic curve’s quaternionic endomorphisms supply the obstruction. The proposed replacement is W(k), or its fraction field, for perfect k.','The manuscript lists only part of the Weil axioms. The explicit obstruction reconstructs faithful endomorphism action and dimension two; it does not deny all Q_p-valued invariants.','agrees','The notes retain perfectness and treat the coefficient ring as a proposal rather than a constructed theory.')
I(56,[150],7,[32],[[1393,1406]],'reading question','groupes de Brauer; ζ','The oblique Brauer/Tate/zeta marginalia suggest a connection to special values and p-primary invariants but do not state a recoverable exact formula.','No precise Tate conjecture or Brauer-group equality is supplied from uncertain connectives.','agrees','The note correctly asks for ink confirmation before mathematical attribution.')
I(57,[152,154,155,156,157],7,[33,34,35],[[1415,1445]],'conjecture','On espère que Raynaud; compléter le tableau','Explore flat-cohomological duality and geometric class field theory in dimension one, compare it with a separately defined p-adic theory, and supply degrees absent from constant p-primary cohomology. The stated Artin bound is H^i(X,Z/p^n)=0 for i>dim X+1 under the finite-dimensional noetherian hypotheses.','Constant finite étale coefficients have the same flat and étale cohomology. The bound is not a bound for every nonconstant finite flat group scheme, and the hoped-for completion is not a defined functor.','agrees','The attribution to Raynaud and the unspecified technical obstacles are reports, not demonstrations of why the problem remained open.')
I(58,[153],7,[33,34,35],[[1408,1455]],'idea of the earlier run','W(k); p-groupes','The earlier run proposes a table of the actual coefficient groups and pairings before comparing the Witt-valued and flat-cohomological programmes.','The task correctly refuses to infer comparison maps from proximity of the two proposals.','agrees','The two programmes are contiguous but explicitly distinguished.')
I(59,[158,159,161,164,165,166,168,169],7,[35,36,37,38],[[1436,1515]],'conjecture','à isom canonique près; au risque de la défigurer','For smooth proper lifts over W(k), de Rham hypercohomology is finite over W(k). Seek canonical independence of algebraic/formal lift and the correct Betti ranks, and report Washnitzer’s oral global weak-lifting site.','Existence of a lift is assumed. The integral finite proper theory is crystalline; ordinary completed de Rham complexes on nonproper lifts behave differently. The author warns that his account may distort Monsky–Washnitzer and distinguishes known rational affine results.','agrees','The earlier notes preserve the report’s uncertainty and separate canonical cohomological invariance from noncanonical isomorphism of lifts.')
I(60,[162,203,204],7,[36,48],[[1472,1490],[1684,1727]],'unproved claim','avec chgt de base; cela montre trivialement','For a smooth quasi-compact quasi-separated morphism, relative de Rham derived direct image obeys derived base change. The passage then treats ordinary cohomology pullback along a section as automatic.','The latter inference needs cohomological flatness or a degenerating Tor spectral sequence. Proper smooth morphisms in characteristic zero have locally free de Rham cohomology and do satisfy ordinary base change; derived compatibility alone is insufficient.','understates','The earlier note flags the cohomology-level arrow but does not isolate the omitted flatness hypothesis. Grothendieck’s published 1968 §1.3 explicitly makes this distinction.',{203:('corrected','Keep derived base change; qualify the ordinary R^i/g* equality by local freeness or the appropriate Tor vanishing.'),204:('kept','Encoding lifts by sections is a valid setup and does not itself establish the comparison.')})
I(61,[163,167,170],7,[36,37,38,18,6,12],[[1457,1515],[1137,1165],[921,1025]],'idea of the earlier run','chgt de base; en tensorisant par K','The earlier run proposes separate audits of integral/torsion/rational invariants, base-change maps and the two proposed sites of liftings.','These are three linked editorial research tasks, each anchored in explicit source maps or omissions; none is a theorem.','agrees','The earlier notes keep all comparison maps as tasks rather than inferred identifications.')
I(62,[171,172,173,174,175,176],7,[39,40],[[1517,1575]],'unproved claim','unique à isom (non unique) près; de rang infini','Smooth affine schemes over a perfect residue field lift formally and smoothly over W(k), uniquely up to a nonunique formal isomorphism. For the completed affine line W{t}, termwise integration of a_n t^n dt gives a_n/(n+1), and H^1 of its continuous de Rham complex has infinite rank over W.','For an integral primitive, all coefficients a_n/(n+1) must lie in W as well as tend to zero. The displayed valuation-limit criterion alone misses the finitely many integrality conditions. A disjoint-support family gives the missing infinite-rank proof.','understates','The note identifies the gap between nonexactness and infinite rank; add the omitted integrality condition. The earlier run’s sparse sequences have been checked.',{175:('corrected','The valuation limit is the convergence criterion over the fraction field. Over W also require v_p(a_n)≥v_p(n+1) for every n.'),174:('kept','The infinite-rank assertion is true; an independent proof is supplied in this run.'),176:('merged','The two sparse valuation examples are valid and are incorporated into the stronger disjoint-support calculation.')})
I(63,[177,179],7,[41],[[1577,1590]],'reading question','|a_n|=O(1)rho^n, rho>1','The written positive-exponent bound does not express overconvergent decay. Convergence on a disk of radius rho>1 instead requires decay comparable to rho^(−n), or an equivalent radius condition.','This is a mathematical diagnostic, not a decided reading. The example a_n=1 satisfies the displayed bound but is not even restricted; a restricted slowly decaying sequence also separates the two conditions.','agrees','The earlier note already spots the sign/radius issue and explicitly leaves the ink undecided.')
I(64,[178,180],7,[41,42],[[1588,1601]],'self-flagged step','les idéaux sont-ils automatiquement fermés','Ask whether ideals in the intended weakly complete overconvergent algebra are automatically closed in the intended topology.','The topology is crucial. Algebraic dagger-algebra ideals are closed for the usual separated induced adic topology under the relevant noetherian hypotheses; an unspecified bornological/direct-limit topology is a separate question.','agrees','The parenthesis genuinely continues across the page break and ends with a question, not an assertion.')
I(65,[181,182,183,184,185,186,187],7,[42,43],[[1603,1644]],'conjecture','Il semble que le théorème suivant soit vrai; ne dépend que de sa réduction','For smooth affine A_0 over a perfect field, seek a smooth weak formal lift A over W, unique up to a nonunique isomorphism; define continuous differential forms and show that a lift of a special-fibre map induces a cohomology map depending only on that map, integrally as well as rationally.','Continuous differentials are the relevant finite module; the exterior-algebra formula omits Ω^1 and the cohomology arrow repeats A′. These are flagged, not silently corrected. The author reports only special affine/rational results available to him.','agrees','The earlier run’s map-recording proposal is incorporated as an audit of the exact invariance claim, with no unproved independence attributed to the earlier run.')
I(66,[188,189,190,191],7,[44],[[1646,1656]],'self-flagged step','Ils vérifient (?)','The reported weak-lifting Zariski site is meant to produce intrinsic integral cohomology by hypercohomology of continuous de Rham complexes, and to agree with formal-lift cohomology in the appropriate proper case.','The site data and comparison theorem are not supplied. The local complex should be over W for the displayed lifts; the written A/k is a reading alert. General nonproper completed cohomology is already refuted on 039–040.','agrees','The explicit question mark prevents certifying the proposed comparison from this page alone.')
I(67,[192,193,194],7,[45],[[1658,1664]],'conjecture','Est-ce la torsion p-adique de la fibre générique ... géométrique ?','Ask whether torsion in a proposed integral p-adic cohomology agrees with p-primary torsion in étale cohomology of the geometric generic fibre of a smooth proper lift; ask for specialization and cycle-family compatibility, motivated by Tate’s degree-one work.','No comparison map or equality is established here. Modern integral comparison imposes torsion inequalities and torsion-free implications; a rational comparison alone cannot answer this integral question.','agrees','The notes correctly separate the torsion question, the specialization programme and the imprecise Inventiones project reference.')
I(68,[195],7,[45],[[1658,1664]],'idea of the earlier run','torsion p-adique','The earlier run proposes a single-variety record of integral de Rham groups, their torsion, generic-fibre étale groups and all comparison maps.','This is a proposed concrete audit, not evidence of equality.','agrees','The proposed comparison is exactly the question asked on the page.')
I(69,[196,197,198,200],7,[46,47],[[1666,1682]],'unproved claim','sans espoir ... Leray; modules de t.f. sur W','The author criticizes a theory based only on de Rham complexes for lacking relative Leray coefficients and reports unproved finiteness, duality and Künneth expectations, while tentatively crediting a Lefschetz–Weil zeta formula. Integral finite generation of general affine Monsky–Washnitzer groups is false already for A^1, whose H^1 has unbounded p-power torsion.','The rational theory and the smooth proper integral theory must be separated. The assertion of hopelessness is a historical assessment, not a mathematical obstruction theorem; the Dwork comparison is explicitly second-hand.','understates','The earlier note accurately reports uncertainty but does not distinguish the false integral finiteness expectation from subsequent rational finiteness results.',{198:('corrected','Finite generation over W fails for integral affine Monsky–Washnitzer cohomology; polynomial classes of orders p^r already occur on A^1.'),196:('kept','Record this as a criticism of the available formalism, not a proved impossibility of relative cohomology.'),197:('kept','The X_t versus X_0 subscript remains an undecided reading.'),200:('kept','The tentative trace-formula report is not a claim that every needed comparison was proved.')})
I(70,[201,202,205,206,207,208],7,[47,48,49],[[1674,1743]],'conjecture','caractère assez surprenant; g=g_0 mod ...','Model different lifts by sections of a parameter scheme and seek canonical, transitive identifications of pullbacks of de Rham cohomology for sections with the same special value, at least after removing torsion.','This requires an actual stratification/convergent Taylor isomorphism and is not a consequence of derived base change. The heuristic heading and a backwards reference remain uncertain.','agrees','The earlier notes preserve the nontriviality and the intended same-special-fibre condition.')
I(71,[209],7,[48,49],[[1684,1743]],'idea of the earlier run','sections; K^bullet','The earlier run proposes comparing pullbacks of one derived complex along two explicit sections and locating the extra structure needed for a canonical isomorphism.','It is a legitimate test of the manuscript’s inference, not a proposed isomorphism without hypotheses.','agrees','Both the family diagram and the demand for canonical comparisons are present.')
I(72,[210,211],7,[50],[[1745,1756]],'conjecture','structure de stratification; connexions intégrables','A transitive system of infinitesimal pullback isomorphisms would give canonical comparisons between nearby liftings; ask how this relates to integrable connections.','Over Q-schemes, suitable stratifications and integrable connections are equivalent. In characteristic p, an arbitrary integrable connection does not extend to ordinary all-order stratification.','agrees','The text asks for the structure and its consequences rather than providing the Taylor construction in mixed characteristic.')
I(73,[212,213,214,216],7,[51],[[1758,1775]],'unproved claim','d i_D+i_D d=Theta_D','Cartan’s homotopy formula makes the Lie derivative of a vertical vector field act trivially on de Rham cohomology; lifting base derivations in a smooth affine family then induces the Gauss–Manin connection, independent of lift and compatible with brackets.','The opening Manin subspace formula repeats H^1(X,Ω^bullet) inside H^1_dR and may have a missing filtration or changed symbol. The final R?f_* notation is not normalized from context alone.','agrees','The explicit homotopy mechanism is valid; the note correctly distinguishes it from the unclear opening formula and promised general appendix.')
I(74,[215],7,[51],[[1758,1775]],'idea of the earlier run','Theta_D','The earlier run proposes a two-variable polynomial check of Cartan’s formula and the independence of cohomology from a vertical lift.','A polynomial identity check supports the algebraic formula, but does not supply sheaf descent or the proper family theorem.','agrees','The elementary identity is independently checked in the saved code.')
I(75,[217,218,219,220,221,222,223],7,[52,53],[[1777,1808]],'withdrawn in the same text','espoir ... faux en car. p ... famille de courbes elliptiques','For smooth families the author proposes a derived absolute connection; for proper smooth families in characteristic zero, Gauss–Manin cohomology is locally free, commutes with base change and carries the analytic-compatible integrable connection. The stronger hope of an ordinary stratification in characteristic p is explicitly corrected by elliptic-family counterexamples.','The withdrawn statement must not be reintroduced as “integrable implies stratified” in characteristic p. A crystalline/PD stratification is a different structure.','agrees','The notes accurately preserve the author’s correction and the three-part programme that follows it.')
I(76,[224],7,[52,53],[[1777,1808]],'idea of the earlier run','famille de courbes elliptiques','The earlier run proposes computing an explicit elliptic Gauss–Manin p-curvature to test the failure of ordinary stratification.','A nonzero p-curvature is an obstruction to an ordinary Frobenius-descended stratification; it does not obstruct the appropriate crystalline connection.','agrees','The proposed diagnostic matches the example named by the author and is labelled as the earlier run’s task.')
I(77,[225,226,227,228,229,230,231],7,[54,55],[[1810,1861]],'unproved claim','s’induisent mutuellement; faisceaux spéciaux','A stratification is a compatible transitive system of pullback identifications along infinitesimally equal maps. For a formally smooth X/S, local lifts exist, the draft stratification and infinitesimal sites agree in the indicated sense, and crystals/cartesian modules correspond to stratified modules. In characteristic zero smooth de Rham cohomology computes their cohomology.','The terminal object is in the sheaf topos, not automatically represented by X in the larger site. Formal smoothness is essential to the local-lift argument, and this draft site has no PD data.','agrees','The notes retain cartesian compatibility and do not replace the two draft topologies by modern crystalline terminology.')
I(78,[232,233],7,[56],[[1863,1876]],'conjecture','Conj ... même si X ... pas lisse','For every scheme locally of finite type over C, including singular X, infinitesimal cohomology of the structure crystal should agree canonically with singular cohomology of X^an.','The smooth case is already established; the singular conjecture is about the infinitesimal site, not naive Ω_X^bullet. For general nonseparated schemes, use the locally finite-type version and its sheaf-theoretic analytification convention.','agrees','The note correctly distinguishes the new singular case from the known smooth comparison.')
I(79,[234],7,[56],[[1863,1876]],'idea of the earlier run','Conj','The earlier run proposes explicit singular affine examples as tests of the infinitesimal-site comparison, keeping naive and completed de Rham complexes separate.','A good test design; no computation of the site is contained in the note.','agrees','The proposal targets the actual conjecture rather than its already-known smooth case.')
I(80,[235,236,237,238,239,240,241,242,243],7,[57,58],[[1878,1932]],'unproved claim','C^n(F)=lim F(...) ; Q^0(F)','The Čech–Alexander complex evaluates a crystal on all finite diagonal neighbourhoods, takes the inverse limit, and computes stratification cohomology under the stated quasi-coherent/flatness conditions. Formalization Q^0 is linked to ordinary Zariski cohomology and extends finite-order differential operators.','The multiple projection pullbacks are identified by the crystal’s transition maps. Inverse limits of sheaves and higher direct images require exactness hypotheses; the displayed f^*f_* analogy is not a general base-change theorem without its assumptions.','agrees','The notes retain the pro-quasi-coherent qualifications and distinguish the analogy from an unconditional equivalence.')
I(81,[250,251,252,253,254],7,[60],[[1962,2013]],'unproved claim','tilde X→e couvrant; lim ind ... Hom','The representable sheaf attached to X covers the final sheaf in the stratification topos. Its Čech nerve is expressed as a filtered colimit of representables of finite diagonal neighbourhoods, leading to the displayed spectral sequence.','A filtered colimit in sheaves is not the same as the inverse system of formal schemes. The last sentence continues on cote7 scan061 of the next package; there is no missing archive passage.','agrees','The earlier note’s truncation is a package boundary, and the pro/ind variance must remain explicit.',{254:('corrected','The clause beginning D’autre part continues at cote7/061 lines1–33 in the next package; no source lacuna should be inferred.')})
I(82,[255],7,[60],[[1962,2013]],'idea of the earlier run','suite spectrale de Cech','The earlier run proposes a low-degree Čech calculation separating the cover in the topos, its representable approximants and the inverse-limit coefficient system.','This is an audit task, not a completed proof of the spectral sequence or its convergence.','agrees','It directly addresses the distinct categorical levels of the displayed construction.')

